#article-introduction[Introduction] <sec:l2009-model-introduction>
#source(1, printed: 185)
A fundamental correspondence between the elementary properties of the
unitriangular group of degree 3 and the coefficient ring was found in
[@bib:l2009-model-Malcev1960]. The model-theoretic study of linear groups and
rings initiated by A.~I. Mal’tsev is closely related to isomorphism theory. Let
$Gamma$ be a chain or a linearly ordered set with order $<=$, and let $K$ be an
associative ring with identity. A $Gamma$-matrix $lr(‖a_(i j)‖)_(i,j in Gamma)$
over $K$ is said to be finitary if it has finitely many nonzero elements. Such
$Gamma$-matrices satisfying the lower niltriangularity condition $a_(i j)=0$ for
$i<=j$ form a ring $NT lr((Gamma,K))$, or $NT lr((n,K))$ (for
$Gamma={1,2,dots,n}$) under ordinary matrix addition and multiplication. Videla
[@bib:l2009-model-Videla1988] interpreted a coefficient ring in the ring
$NT lr((n,K))$ with $n>2$ and transferred there the elementary equivalence as
$NT lr((n,K)) equiv NT lr((m,S)) <==> n=m$, and $K equiv S$, for nonzero
coefficient rings $K$ and $S$, on the basis of the Mal’tsev correspondence and
the description of the automorphisms of the ring $NT lr((n,K))$ given in
[@bib:l2009-model-Levchuk1975, @bib:l2009-model-Levchuk1983]. (The case of
coefficient fields was considered in, e.g., [@bib:l2009-model-Rose1978].)

The mapping $alpha arrow e+alpha$ of the ring $R=NT lr((Gamma,K))$ is an
isomorphism of its adjoint group, which is isomorphic to the unitriangular group
$UT lr((Gamma,K))$. Let $Lambda lr((R))$ and $J lr((R))$ denote, respectively,
the Lie and Jordan rings associated with $R$. The automorphisms of
$Lambda lr((R))$ and $UT lr((Gamma,K))$ were described in
[@bib:l2009-model-Levchuk1983] in the case where $3<|Gamma|<infinity$ or
$|Gamma|=3$ and the ring $K$ is commutative; for a ring $K$ without zero
divisors, they were described for any chain $Gamma$ in
[@bib:l2009-model-Levchuk1987]. Isomorphisms in the same cases were constructed
in [@bib:l2009-model-KuzucuogluLevchuk2004]. In Sections
@sec:l2009-model-derivations and @sec:l2009-model-isomorphisms, we find Jordan
isomorphisms in a similar way; we also extend theorems of Herstein
[@bib:l2009-model-Herstein1956, @bib:l2009-model-Herstein1957] on Lie and Jordan
derivations. Special cases were considered in, e.g., [@bib:l2009-model-Wang2007,
@bib:l2009-model-ChunPark2006, @bib:l2009-model-OuWangYao2007].

Section @sec:l2009-model-elementary-equivalence studies the relationship between
the elementary equivalences $Lambda lr((R)) equiv Lambda lr((R'))$ for
$R'=NT lr((m,S))$ and $UT lr((n,K)) equiv UT lr((m,S))$ (the case of a
commutative ring $K$ was studied in [@bib:l2009-model-Belegradek1999,
@bib:l2009-model-Minakova2008]), as well as $J lr((R)) equiv J lr((R'))$, with
the elementary properties of the base rings and the interpretability of
coefficient rings.
